FortiGate Manager for Debian 13 amd64
===================================

Install using apt so dependencies are resolved:

    sudo apt install ./fortigate-manager_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb

Open Internet -> FortiGate Manager, or run /usr/bin/fortigate-manager.
The client starts in the system tray. Its Open action shows the window;
/usr/bin/fortigate-manager --show opens it immediately.

The package enables fortigate-manager.socket. The local service starts when
the client connects to that socket and depends on strongswan.service.
Installation or a changed route plugin restarts strongSwan and disconnects
its existing tunnels. Application upgrades stop the manager's own tunnels.

When installed using sudo, the sudo user is added to the fortigate-manager
access group. For a graphical/root installation or another desktop account:

    sudo fortigate-manager-access USER

The launcher refreshes the authorized group for its process. If group access
still fails, sign out and back in. Do not run the GUI as root.

Saved profiles and PSKs remain under /var/lib/fortigate-manager, with root-only
permissions. No saved profiles, PSKs, tokens, or personal VPN details are
included in this package. Removal and purge retain saved data for recovery.

Known, unmodified service/socket units from the previous manual installer are
backed up under /var/lib/fortigate-manager/manual-unit-backups and removed
from /etc/systemd/system so the packaged units are used. Modified overrides
are preserved. Old /usr/local binaries are not overwritten: use the Internet
menu or /usr/bin/fortigate-manager after installing this package. Do not use
the old manual uninstall script to remove the Debian package.

The installed plugin is specific to strongSwan 6.0.1; dependencies enforce
that version range. The current implementation supports FortiGate IKEv2,
PSK plus browser SAML, Mode Config, UDP transport and advertised IPv4 routes.
It does not implement an SSL VPN or an IPv6 VPN.

Useful checks:
    systemctl status fortigate-manager.socket fortigate-manager.service
    sudo /usr/libexec/fortigate-managerd --check
    ip route show table 220

Remove with: sudo apt remove fortigate-manager
Established connections belong to the service, not to the GUI: quitting the
tray app leaves them connected until explicitly disconnected.
